course: dictionary
lesson: 15659
title: Thickly: one word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one you need. Thickly is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done. «Spread the butter thickly» tells you how the butter was spread.
explain: The same word can mean different things. «The forest was thickly wooded» means the trees grow close together. «Snow fell thickly» means a lot of snow came down, very close together. Look at the words around thickly to find its meaning.
explain: Thickly can also describe how someone speaks. «After crying, Sam spoke thickly» means his words came out unclear and hoarse, as when someone has a cold or is very upset.
fact: Thickly is an adverb, so it tells you how an action happens.
fact: «Thickly» can mean in a thick layer, as in «spread the jam thickly».
fact: «Thickly» can mean closely together, as in «thickly wooded hills» or «thickly populated» areas.
fact: «Thickly» can describe speech that is unclear or hoarse, as in «He spoke thickly with a cold».
fact: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If «spread in a thick layer» fits, you have found the right meaning.
exercise: «Mum spread the jam thickly on the toast.» How was the jam spread? | Slowly, one drop at a time | In a thin, see-through layer | *In a thick layer | Quickly, in a rush
exercise: «The forest was thickly wooded.» What does thickly mean here? | Covered in snow | *Closely together, with many trees | Very quietly | Far apart, with only a few trees
exercise: «After crying, Sam spoke thickly.» How did Sam speak? | *Unclearly, in a hoarse voice | Politely, in a whisper | Very fast, with lots of jokes | Loudly and happily
test: «The fields were thickly covered in snow.» What does thickly mean here? | *In a thick layer | Brightly lit | Not at all | Very slowly
test: «The city is thickly populated.» What does thickly mean here? | With no people at all | Very quietly | In a thin layer | *With many people living close together
exam: Which sentence uses thickly to mean unclear or hoarse speech? | She sliced the cake thickly. | The hills were thickly wooded. | *He mumbled thickly, with a bad cold. | Snow fell thickly on the roof.
